<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dies irae</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century at the latest (manuscript of <a href="/years/1253">1253</a>–1255)</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dies irae, &quot;the Day of Wrath&quot;, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> sequence, a hymn of the Mass. It is attributed either to the Franciscan Thomas of Celano (1200–1265) or to Latino Malabranca Orsini (d. 1294), lector at the Dominican house of studies at Santa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabina">Sabina</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>; some sources take it back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Gregory the Great</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-of-clairvaux">Bernard of Clairvaux</a> or Bonaventure. It dates from the 13th century at the latest.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Dies irae</b>, &quot;the Day of Wrath&quot;, is a Latin sequence, a hymn of the Mass.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dies-irae">Dies irae</a></li><li><blockquote>The Roman Missal of the Tridentine Mass, published in 1570 after the council, kept only four medieval sequences: this one, &quot;Veni Sancte Spiritus&quot; for Pentecost, &quot;Lauda Sion&quot; for Corpus Christi and &quot;<b>Dies irae</b>&quot; for the Requiem Mass.</blockquote><a href="/songs/victimae-paschali-laudes">Victimae paschali laudes</a></li></ul></section>
